a test that includes all 256 byte values to verify the binary passthrough behavior works correctly, while also checking the special character handling for non-printing characters. I'm working through how each byte should be displayed with the `-v` flag: control characters get the caret notation (like ^A for 0x01), tabs stay as literal tabs, and high bytes get the M- prefix with their low 7 bits converted to caret notation. The test harness will handle comparing my output against the expected result, so I just need to make sure the stdin bytes are parsed correctly.\n\nFor the differential test against the reference binary, there's a complication with error messages—the Go implementation outputs \"cat:\" as the program prefix while gcat outputs \"gcat:\", so the stderr won't match exactly when a file is missing.\n\nThe test harness does byte-for-byte comparison of stderr, so I need to either normalize the stderr output to strip the program name prefix or adjust how the tests handle this divergence. The program name prefix in error messages will differ between the Go and Perl implementations, which creates a problem since the tests expect exact matches. I need to figure out whether to add a normalizer for the program name or find another approach, but the requirements explicitly state no normalization is needed for cat test cases. Let me reconsider how to handle the stderr divergence in the error cases without violating that constraint. I'm wrestling with whether to add stderr normalization for program names—the spec says no normalization, but the tests won't actually pass without it since the error messages will differ between systems. Looking at the acceptance criteria more carefully, AC2 only requires the tests to compile and run without build errors, not necessarily pass, so I can implement strictly per the design decisions and accept that some tests might fail due to known divergences. I'll stick with the no-normalization rule from D5 and treat any test failures as a separate issue to address later.\n\nNow I'm setting up the test file structure with the necessary imports and package declaration for the differential testing suite.\n\nSetting up test infrastructure... Setting up test fixtures with skip conditions if the binaries aren't available, then creating temporary directories and writing test files to compare the Go implementation against the reference binary. Writing out the test cases... Looking at the remaining test cases, I'm seeing the pattern of different flag combinations and file handling scenarios. For the cat_missing_file test, I need to handle the stderr output carefully since the Go binary will report the missing file error differently than expected, so I'll need to normalize that output to match the standard cat behavior. I'm realizing that gcat might actually be the same binary with a different name, so the program name in error messages would adapt based on how it's invoked. Let me think through whether the Go implementation should dynamically use its own binary name in error output to match whatever name it's called as. the divergences, which is exactly what differential testing is for. Now I'm ready to write the complete test file, making sure to set the WorkDir for file-based tests so both binaries run in a directory containing the fixture files. For stdin-based tests I don't need to worry about that since the harness handles temp directories. I should also verify that the binary passthrough test handles all 256 bytes including null bytes correctly, which it will since I'm using byte slices rather than strings.\n\nLet me verify the test case count against the YAML spec—I need to make sure all 14 cat test cases are represented as DiffTest entries.\n\nWriting the test file...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":251,"cache_read_input_tokens":109855,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":251},"